tions derived from the pretrained pan-cancer model of XunZi-M with NSCLC-specific multi-omics datasets", "carrier_family": "dense_continuous_carrier", "carrier_subtype": "pooled_or_aggregated_embedding", "insertion_or_fusion_verbatim": "we integrated the gene-level representations derived from the pretrained pan-cancer model of XunZi-M with NSCLC-specific multi-omics datasets", "evidence_quote": "To evaluate the utility of XunZi, we selected two complex diseases: NSCLC and PD. Novel therapeutic targets are urgently required for both diseases due to their substantially unmet clinical needs 36-39 . High-quality multi-omics datasets of the two diseases are available from public databases and/or published literature. Focusing on NSCLC, we integrated the gene-level representations derived from the pretrained pan-cancer model of XunZi-M with NSCLC-specific multi-omics datasets, to fine-tune a model for the prediction of NSCLC-specific regulators. Using fivefold cross-validation, XunZi achieved a higher AUC value of 0.86, compared with AUC values of 0.65, 0.72 and 0.70 for GPT-4o, DNNs and SVMs (Fig. 2a). XunZi also achieved a higher AUPRC value than other methods (Supplementary Fig. 1a) and showed superior performance in the reciprocal test (Supplementary Fig. 1b-f). The t -distributed", "section_heading": "XunZi outperforms existing methods in predicting regulators underlying NSCLC and PD", "supporting_figure_or_table": "Fig. 2a"}]}

